1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:06,055
This is how I built an alarm clock as a Python command-line app, for the build exercise.

2
00:00:06,055 --> 00:00:08,111
The brief was deliberately open:

3
00:00:08,111 --> 00:00:10,874
no spec, no database, decide what to build.

4
00:00:10,874 --> 00:00:12,738
So the first job wasn't code.

5
00:00:12,738 --> 00:00:14,537
It was defining the problem.

6
00:00:15,837 --> 00:00:24,840
Before writing anything, I had Claude ask me clarifying questions, in two short rounds, and I made the decisions that shape everything else.

7
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:28,891
Alarms are saved to a file, and a foreground runner rings them.

8
00:00:28,891 --> 00:00:33,264
The scope is recurring days, snooze and dismiss, and editing alarms;

9
00:00:33,264 --> 00:00:34,807
relative timers are out.

10
00:00:34,807 --> 00:00:42,909
If the laptop was asleep, a late alarm still rings within a fifteen-minute grace window, and is reported as missed after that.

11
00:00:42,909 --> 00:00:45,546
Twice I overrode Claude's recommendation:

12
00:00:45,546 --> 00:00:53,005
I wanted sound on all three operating systems, not just macOS, and I chose Typer and Rich over the standard library.

13
00:00:54,305 --> 00:01:00,082
Claude then wrote an implementation plan, which I approved, and built it in ten milestones:

14
00:01:00,082 --> 00:01:03,319
one commit each, with the tests in the same commit.

15
00:01:03,319 --> 00:01:05,160
The core is a pure scheduler.

16
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:13,095
It never reads the clock, so a whole morning, a closed laptop lid, or a daylight-saving change can be tested in milliseconds.

17
00:01:13,095 --> 00:01:19,950
And each alarm remembers the last time it handled, so nothing rings twice, even if the clock goes backwards.

18
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:23,764
I chose how the result would be validated:

19
00:01:23,764 --> 00:01:29,811
fakes for anything platform-specific, CI on three operating systems, and a written validation report.

20
00:01:29,811 --> 00:01:40,467
There are a hundred and eighty-four tests, including property-based tests and fake-clock scenarios, plus two end-to-end tests that drive the real program through a real terminal.

21
00:01:40,467 --> 00:01:44,777
Claude also added a mutation check that injects fifteen deliberate bugs.

22
00:01:44,777 --> 00:01:48,788
When one of them first slipped through, the weak test was replaced.

23
00:01:48,788 --> 00:01:50,225
Now every one is caught.

24
00:01:51,525 --> 00:01:53,470
The checks found real problems.

25
00:01:53,470 --> 00:01:55,916
Some were design pitfalls caught early;

26
00:01:55,916 --> 00:01:58,927
others were mistakes in Claude's first attempts.

27
00:01:58,927 --> 00:02:08,211
Python compares two times in the same time zone by clock reading alone, which breaks across daylight-saving changes, so every time is stored in UTC.

28
00:02:08,211 --> 00:02:12,602
Type-checking the code as Windows caught a failure before CI ever ran.

29
00:02:12,602 --> 00:02:16,993
And the first real CI run failed on two things that only happen there:

30
00:02:16,993 --> 00:02:21,948
forced colour output on GitHub, and Windows releasing file locks a moment late.

31
00:02:21,948 --> 00:02:23,830
Both are fixed and written up.

32
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:28,670
Then Claude ran a structured code review of its own code.

33
00:02:28,670 --> 00:02:29,913
It found ten issues.

34
00:02:29,913 --> 00:02:34,820
Nine were fixed, each with a regression test confirmed to fail on the old code.

35
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:42,708
For example, a grace window of zero meant alarms never rang, and two alarm commands run at the same moment could lose an alarm;

36
00:02:42,708 --> 00:02:46,187
that one failed three times out of three before the fix.

37
00:02:46,187 --> 00:02:52,212
One finding, a time-zone change while the runner is open, was consciously skipped and documented.

38
00:02:53,512 --> 00:02:54,601
Here's the result.

39
00:02:54,601 --> 00:02:56,720
Two alarms ring in the same minute:

40
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:01,261
the first is snoozed, the second dismissed, and a second runner is refused.

41
00:03:17,012 --> 00:03:17,489
On time:

42
00:03:17,489 --> 00:03:23,990
from the first question to green CI took about sixty-six minutes, roughly double the thirty-minute guideline.

43
00:03:23,990 --> 00:03:31,803
That was just over twenty minutes defining the problem and the plan, about thirty-five building, and the rest fixing what CI found.

44
00:03:31,803 --> 00:03:35,084
I chose to spend it on validation rather than features.

45
00:03:35,084 --> 00:03:38,543
With more time, I'd fix one small thing the demo revealed:

46
00:03:38,543 --> 00:03:42,778
the runner still counts a finished one-time alarm as one it's watching.

47
00:03:42,778 --> 00:03:44,329
Everything is in the repo:

48
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:49,160
the decision log, the validation report, and the scripts that build these videos.

49
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:55,959
This narration is a synthetic voice reading a script Claude drafted from the project history, and that I reviewed.
